the el-fish loom roundabout is in the  heart of tripoli on most days during the  year hundreds of african laborers can be  found here looking for work  but during the holy month of ramadan  things slow down and the crowds thin  abdul rahim is a painter from niger he  says he came to libya two years ago to  find work and provide for his family  back home  during ramadan work is hard to come by  many of us don't make enough money to be  able to buy food i have friends who  haven't eaten for days but some people  do try to help  the un says there are more than 610  000 migrants in libya it's long been a  transit hub for african migrants trying  to reach europe the international  organization for migration says at least  660 people died risking the dangerous  journey in the mediterranean  sea  and nearly 900 are missing some migrants  such as sophian from sudan are not  interested in going to europe  they seek economic opportunities in  libya  some of us have family members who are  sick and need to make money for their  treatment there is little work during  ramadan it's hard for us i need to work  so i can eat and send money to my family  ramadan is the holiest month for muslims  they fast from sunrise until sunset and  believe it's a time for giving an act of  charity  many migrants depend on the goodwill of  libyans such as hamid al-dibh who  started the farikh san charity  organization seven years ago  during ramadan labourers don't find work  so we try to help them and give them  food it's our obligation as muslims to  help those in need  libya has been engulfed in conflict and  political divisions for more than a  decade  but migrants still risk coming here  because there are no job opportunities  at home  although difficult  and at times dangerous for some it's  their only hope to provide for  themselves and their families malik  trainer al jazeera tripoli
 

